From 07d54bfd5f38d066a8b240d59b3fcec1e9912f4a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Medium1992 <122653016+Medium1992@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 18:15:26 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] XHTTP: Shrink HTTP/2 flow windows when the reader slows
 down

A window grown by a fast reader stayed at that size, so a reader that
settled to a lower rate, such as a video player once its buffer is
full, kept the fast-phase amount unread. A window now comes down by a
quarter at a time, never below its initial size, once its reader has
consumed less than a quarter of it per round trip for three round trips
in a row. It still grows at once when the reader speeds up.

Downlink windows on a server that has had to unstick a client which
returns credit in large steps are left alone, so the guard and the
shrinking do not undo each other.

Throughput assertions are skipped under the race detector, where the
extra copying costs enough CPU to make them meaningless.
